findings.statement:
A debug tool's identity is a bare string repeated across lists in two languages:
- the ToolDef registry (69 names);
- either a Rust dispatch_tool match arm (21) or, through the string fall-through `_ => query_frontend(name, …)`, a bridge.ts handler (48);
- test/visual/assertions.ts KNOWN_DEBUG_TOOL_NAMES;
- debugParity.test.ts PURE_ENGINE_SIDE;
- gui/sidecar/src/system-prompt.ts (17 names; no parity check at all, though all match today).

fixture_relpath also has a TS twin ('Add new fixtures to BOTH sides'). Agreement between the lists is enforced by regex-parsing debug_server.rs as text: debugParity.test.ts:72 matches `"name" =>` arms, and debug_write_tool_routing_tests.rs (1,194 lines plus a 567-line fixture corpus) is a hand-written Rust lexer. The registry comment lists four places to edit per new tool. Its in-code pointer to the registry '(:1019-1024)' is stale; the reify_* defs now sit at 1210-1300.

After building the wire DTOs, engine logic reads them back as input through their string fields:
- surface_geometry_derived_cells skips cells where `cell.determinacy == "determined"`, rebuilds `ValueCellId::new(&cell.entity_path, &cell.name)` from DTO strings, and overwrites value, unit, determinacy, freshness ('final') and si_value in place;
- recheck_indeterminate_constraints formats every constraint id with write!() to match `c.node_id` strings and compares `c.status` against satisfaction_token;
- build_values gates last_substantive_value on `freshness == "pending"`.

The code's own comment admits the hazard: 'a bare literal out of step with it would disable this entire re-check with no compile error'. The typed DeterminacyState, Freshness and Satisfaction values exist one step earlier and are discarded at format_determinacy / format_freshness / satisfaction_token.

engine.rs prose has measurably drifted.

**Stale line-number pointers.** 9 of its 16 line-number pointers point at the wrong code:
- '(:3259-3265)' for resolve_source, now at 3563 (pointer added 2026-08-31);
- 'engine.rs:30-44' for the atomic-commit invariant, which is now import lines;
- '(:3515-3529)' for collect_display_routing, now at 5746;
- 'commands.rs:539', which now points at a JSON projection;
- 'engine_build.rs:5440-5465' and ':5415-5424' for the DELTA CONTRACT, which is now at engine_build.rs:6101;
- 'module_dag.rs:607' for compile_project_with_entry_source, now at 677;
- 'analysis.rs:106' for set_capture_undef_causes, which is at 123.

**Misplaced doc blocks.** Three `///` blocks sit above the wrong item, so rustdoc and hover attach them to a neighbour:
- build_values' doc ('Build the Vec<ValueData>…# Freshness') runs into display_scalar's;
- build_template_node's doc ('Build an EntityTreeNode…# Preconditions') runs into collect_consumed_sibling_names';
- emit_auto_resolve_if_any's doc runs into drain_and_emit_warm_pool_events'.

As a result, build_values, build_template_node and emit_auto_resolve_if_any have no doc of their own.

`--since` is advertised in `print_usage` as 'Window sweep from ISO date (all detectors)'. The /audit skill (modes.md §3) records `{"window": "<iso-date>..now"}` as the run's scope. In fact the flag scopes nothing:
- the bin turns it into `AuditContext::window`, which no detector filters on;
- the fused-memory loader takes no date and always pulls the whole corpus;
- the only reader of `ctx.window` is P2's unbounded-backlog guard, which tests `window.is_none()`. Passing `--since` therefore disables that guard while leaving the backlog unbounded, as p2_consumer_stub.rs's own KNOWN LIMITATION comment admits.
`AuditContext::producer_branch` has no reader at all; its doc says it is kept because ~50 construction sites set it. AuditContext has all-pub fields and no constructor, so every field change edits every struct literal.

`check_one`, the P5 sweep corroboration, is a 410-line rescue ladder with 12 `return`s and 9 inline `Finding` literals (7 of them Low). Two rescues are spelled twice, once in the merged/no-event arm and once in the git-diff leg, in different orders and with near-identical summary text: the task-referencing-commit rescue and the deliverable-presence rescue (`all_files_tracked_on_main`). The `RunsDb{events, task_id=… AND event_type=task_completed}` evidence literal appears 5 times.
The file also holds three different 'does a landed path cover a declared metadata.files entry' predicates:
- the sweep's `files_missing_from`: exact string membership;
- the pre-done gate's `covers_path`: trailing-slash-trimmed and directory-prefix aware;
- task_rows::path_present_in_tracked: the same as covers_path, against the tracked set.
`covers_path`'s doc describes exactly the trailing-slash/directory bug that the sweep path still has.

reify-eval's 84 top-level modules contain one strongly connected component of 24 modules totalling 95,192 raw lines: cache, cell_commit, compute_persist, demand, deps, dirty, engine_admin, engine_build, engine_compute, engine_edit, engine_eval, engine_fixpoint, freshness_walk, geometry_ops, invariants, journal, observed_demand, realization_content, realization_staleness, resolve_order, shell_extract_compute, undef_tracer, unfold and warm_pool. The dependency/cache stratum would sit below the engine-phase modules but for 13 upward reference sites in 8 modules. Nearly all of them fetch pure predicates over CompiledExpr/CompiledGeometryOp that happen to be defined inside engine-phase files:
- deps.rs:399 -> geometry_ops::is_geometry_query_call;
- invariants.rs:337-351 -> 4 geometry_ops predicates (is_feature_datum_projection_call, consumes_geometry_or_selector, is_symbolic_eval_wired_selector_ctor, is_geometry_consumer_call);
- resolve_order.rs:709,808 -> engine_eval::expand_solver_position_expr and is_optimized_userfn_cell;
- realization_staleness.rs:79,191 -> engine_build::compute_realization_upstream_values_hash_from_ops and sub_refs_in_op;
- dirty.rs:282 -> engine_fixpoint::run_unified_pass_seeded;
- geometry_ops.rs:12914 -> engine_build::realize_sub_override_handles;
- realization_content.rs:21 and shell_extract_compute.rs:49 -> contract types through the engine_compute re-export shim.
The predicates live in 13,242-line geometry_ops.rs and 15,307-line engine_eval.rs. Understanding why deps.rs records an edge therefore means loading the engine. The run's import-graph instrument reported no Rust cycle here because it does not follow inline crate:: paths.

Compute targets have no stratum of their own. Each target is split between reify-stdlib and reify-eval according to which crate may hold the solver dependency, not by purpose.

reify-stdlib holds the 'pure' halves:
- modal/trampoline.rs (324 lines, ModalCacheKey/TransientCacheKey), whose doc says the faer/solver-holding half lives in reify-eval 'because reify-stdlib does not depend on faer / reify-solver-elastic';
- modal/free_vibration.rs and modal/transient.rs;
- dynamics/trampoline.rs (298 lines);
- trajectory/trampoline.rs (3,053 lines).

reify-eval holds the solver halves: modal_ops, dynamics_ops, trajectory_ops, dynamics_psd, shell_extract_compute and the 16 compute_targets files, 18,226 production lines in total, plus all 18 register_compute_fn calls.

Those eval halves reach the engine through only four seams:
- persistent_cache, mostly re-exported contract types;
- the solver_progress thread-local;
- topology_selectors, for selector resolution and kernel-reply parsing;
- &mut Engine, for registration.

The split still makes reify-eval carry reify-fdm (used only by 4 compute_targets files) and reify-solver-elastic (8 of its 13 using files are analysis). The generic selector service topology_selectors imports compute_targets::result_topology::CarriedTopology, which closes the cycle compute_targets <-> topology_selectors <-> selector_vocabulary_v2. reify-compute-contract was extracted (#4934) for a 'future reify-eval-fea' crate that does not exist.

The machine-checked layer model covers only the compilation spine. assert-crate-dag.sh (B1-B6, run by crates/reify-build-utils/tests/crate_dag_assertion.rs) constrains 4 of the 36 workspace packages: core, ast, ir and syntax. PLAYER's .jcodemunch.jsonc assigns 13 crate src dirs to 7 layers, emits only Low findings, and leaves 23 packages unassigned. The unassigned set includes every kernel, reify-solver-elastic, reify-stdlib, reify-constraints, reify-compute-contract, reify-mesh-morph, reify-test-support, reify-audit, reify-fdm, reify-gcode, reify-shell-extract and reify-geometry.

Every inversion this seat measured lies in that unassigned band:
- compute-contract -> solver-elastic;
- solver-elastic -> kernel-gmsh;
- kernel-occt -> (optional) mesh-morph -> eval;
- audit -> test-support -> eval;
- expr -> fdm -> gcode;
- constraints -> stdlib (re-export only).

The layer file's own comment for expr ('depends on core + ast + ir + stdlib') already omits reify-fdm. Intra-crate module cycles, such as reify-eval's 24-module SCC, are checked by nothing.
